                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Neon Space 2 — fun for some time, but not a good game[/h1]\n\n[b]TL;DR: Not recommended. Fine as a quick achievement grab for like 80% achievements, but frustrating as an actual game — especially on Steam Deck.[/b]\n\n[h2]Steam Deck: the controls are the biggest problem[/h2]\n\nThe default control scheme simply did not work for me. I had to switch to the \"keyboard WASD + mouse\" template that's offered as a preset, and only then was the game playable at all. Even after that it stays weird and clunky — nothing about steering the ship feels precise or natural.\n\nThis is fixable and it's the single thing that would most improve the game. Anything that requires precision needs controls that don't fight you.\n\n[h2]The abilities are great — the fact that I had to discover them isn't[/h2]\n\nI genuinely like the blink and the slow-motion abilities. They're the best part of the game and they make the good levels feel good.\n\nBut I had to figure both of them out by myself. There's no real explanation of your abilities anywhere I could find. In a strange way that was almost charming — teaching yourself the mechanics through trial and error, a bit like teaching yourself an instrument. But unless a game explicitly frames that as its design, it isn't a feature. It's a gap in development.\n\n[h2]Red walls: no visual distinction between \"pass through\" and \"instant death\"[/h2]\n\nThis is my main design complaint. Some red walls are meant to be passed using blink. Others are placed in the level explicitly as blink-blocking obstacles that kill you.\n\nThere is [b]no visible difference between the two.[/b] None. You find out which is which by dying.\n\nThe maze levels are where this becomes genuinely miserable — and the maze made entirely of red killing walls was pure suffering with zero fun attached.\n\n[h2]The laser level[/h2]\n\nSomewhere in the 25–35 range of the 50 levels there's a stage with extremely fast laser shots that I found confusing and hard to read. Some shots stop mid-air with no clear visual explanation for why; others travel all the way to the wall on the left. There's no consistency you can learn, so you're not reacting to a system, you're just guessing.\n\n[h2]Duplicate achievements[/h2]\n\n\"Unlock 50 medals\" and \"Finish the game\" are literally the same requirement. Two separate achievements for one action is pointless. Most of the achievement list is good — this one is just odd. If the 50 medals were actually meant as in each gained medal is a medal (so level 1 has 3 medals to gain: Bronce, silver, gold) I would have had the achievement by now - also the amount of people that have both achievements is the exact same. So, yeah.\n\n[h2]\"The highly anticipated sequel to Neon Space\"[/h2]\n\nHighly anticipated by whom? I'd never heard of this game before. I got it as a side item in some bundle years ago. Self-applied hype is a strange choice on a store page.\n\n[h2]Let's talk about the price[/h2]\n\nThis game costs [b]€30[/b]. Thirty euros. For a game that takes only like 2 hours of gameplay, has controls that don't work well on Steam Deck, and doesn't explain its own mechanics.\n\nRight now it's on sale for around €1.50, which is a 95% discount. And here's the thing: [b]I don't recommend it at €1.50 either.[/b] Not because €1.50 is explicitly a lot of money, but because your time is worth more than what several of these levels do with it. The sale price isn't a bargain, it's just a smaller mistake.\n\n[h2]What I actually enjoyed[/h2]\n\nThe speedrun vibe of hunting gold medals is genuinely fun, and a good number of levels are enjoyable to optimize. Grabbing 80% of the achievements fairly quickly was satisfying. For what it is, it was a pleasant enough way to pass the time.\n\n[h2]What it would take to fix[/h2]\n\n[list]\n[*] Substantially clearer, better controls — [b]especially on Steam Deck[/b]\n[*] Visual distinction between passable and lethal red walls\n[*] Rework or remove the confusing levels — or at minimum, let players [b]skip[/b] a level they can't or don't want to do\n[*] Actually explain the abilities\n[*] Clean up the duplicate achievement\n[/list]\n\n[h2]Verdict[/h2]\n\n[b]Not recommended.[/b] It has good ideas — blink, slow-mo, medal hunting — buried under controls that don't work, levels that don't communicate their own rules, and no explanation of anything. A nice enough time-killer if you already own it, but I can't tell anyone to go get it.",
